[DONE] Patrols should have all members walk at the same speed — the slowest of
their party. Sometimes one super fast squad member completely outpaces the rest,
and gets destroyed when they encounter an enemy.
    Proof in tools/pace.js. 1 red on the build before, 0 after, with three
    controls that only do real work once the band exists.
    MEASURED FIRST. Four bodies at 1.18x, 1.0x, 1.0x and 0.80x, one order,
    forty tiles of open waste: 7.1 tiles of spread on average and 13.3 at the
    worst of it. The tail was a third of the march behind the head, which is
    exactly the report — the fast one arrives, meets what is waiting, and is
    the only one there.
    THE FAULT IS THAT AN ORDER WAS NEVER A THING. `movers.forEach(routeTo)` is
    five independent walks that happen to share a destination. Nothing in the
    game held the idea "these five are marching together", so there was nothing
    for a pace rule to attach to, and no harness could see a problem because
    every unit involved was doing precisely what it was told.
    So a BAND is the order. `formBand` stamps the bodies given one order at
    once, `clearOrders` drops it, and `paceBands` — one pass at the top of
    `update` — caps every member at the slowest walking member's free speed.
    `moveSpeed` splits into `moveSpeedRaw` (what a body would do unaided) and a
    thin cap on top, so the pass can ask the first without asking itself the
    second one frame deep.
    A BAND THAT SURVIVES CONTACT IS THE SAME BUG WEARING A HAT. A body with a
    target neither takes the cap nor sets it: you cannot drag a man out of a
    melee at ox speed, and a pinned man's crawl must not become the whole
    party's pace. Same for a body that has arrived — it stops setting the floor
    the moment it stops walking.
    AND PATROLS ARE NOT STAMPED. The job re-issues its own legs every few
    seconds and any stamp would be wiped within the second, so `paceKey` gives
    the whole player watch one derived band instead. It is the one case where
    the band is a roster rather than an order, and it is that way because the
    job is.
    After: 1.6 tiles at the worst, and the march takes 14s against 13s — the
    ox's honest pace, not a crawl. Both halves are asserted, because pacing
    everybody to nearly zero is the cheap way to pass the first one.
    ORDERS DO NOT ROUND-TRIP THROUGH A SAVE (`moveTarget` is not written), so
    the band deliberately does not either. Reload mid-march and the party walks
    at its own speeds until you re-order it.

