[DONE] Faction: the Mercenary's Guild. Right now dust mages, destruction
alchemists and necromancers wander the wastes freely. The Guild is where they
unite under one banner, taking jobs freely. Neutral by design, but licenses
soldiers to individual cities for warfare, never against each other. Their own
bastion on the map, similar to the Paladins. Players can hire a crew for big
jobs, paid per day.
    Proof in tools/guild.js.
    A FACTION IS ALMOST IMPOSSIBLE TO ADD WRONG IN A WAY ANYTHING NOTICES. The
    yard stands, the people are in it, the screen opens — and the one clause that
    makes it a guild rather than a gang can be quietly false forever. So the
    weight of the harness is on the two claims that could be.
    NEVER AGAINST EACH OTHER IS ONE LINE, AND IT IS ASKED FIRST. `guildOath(a) &&
    guildOath(b)` sits above every other enmity in `hostile()`, so there is no
    path through the rest of that function that can put two oaths on opposite
    sides — checked at peace, at war, PROVOKED (which is how a neutral becomes an
    enemy everywhere else in this game, and the one that would have broken it),
    and mid-rebellion. Ironscar can license a company and so can Copperhold, and
    when the two armies meet the contingents stand and look at each other while
    the levies go at it around them.
    AND THE CONTROL IS THE OTHER HALF: they still go for the enemy's ordinary
    watch. A charter that made them harmless would pass everything above.
    THE YARD IS NOT THE BASTION. The Bastion is a fortress with a gate and an
    order behind it; this is a walled yard with the gate standing wide, because
    the Guild is not defending anything — it is a place to be found. Sited 40+
    tiles from any town and 70+ from the Bastion: a yard in somebody's suburbs is
    a district, not a faction.
    AND IT IS THE PEOPLE WHO WERE ALREADY OUT THERE. Three flamewrights, two
    dusters, two necromancers and a company of blades — the same archetypes that
    were walking the roads alone, standing in a yard. A guild of anonymous swords
    would have satisfied every other assertion here and would not be what was
    asked for, so the harness counts them by art.
    A CREW IS NOT A RECRUIT AND THE DIFFERENCE IS ONE WORD: WAGE. Three contracts
    at the registrar (who is a VENDOR, which is not a shortcut but the right
    shape — every approach-and-open path already runs through `tradeTarget`, so
    hiring uses the same walk, range check and modal plumbing as buying a
    hammer). Paid at dawn out of the purse; miss it and the whole payroll walks
    back to the yard and it costs 25 standing. No grace and no debt: a crew that
    stays on half wages has decided something about you, and this lot have not
    decided anything.
    AND THEY DO NOT FILL THE BANNER. `SQUAD_CAP` is what a banner holds — people
    who are yours. Counting a crew against it would make the whole feature a
    worse way of hiring a recruit, which is the one thing it must not be. Five
    places asked "is the banner full" and three asked it differently — two
    counted settlers and one did not — so the cap you hit depended on which door
    you walked through. `bannerLoad()` is the one function now.
    STANDING IS A DISCOUNT AND NEVER A SURCHARGE. A Guild that charges you more
    for being unreliable is a Guild that has decided not to work with you, and
    refusal is what that decision looks like — the two better contracts are
    locked behind standing rather than priced out of reach.
    A LICENSED COMPANY HAS TO BE ABLE TO MEET ANOTHER ONE, which took a second
    pass. A town at war fights in TWO places and they are different factions: the
    levy that marches is `warband`, the watch that holds the wall is `town`. Both
    contingents in the same faction could never have met at all, and the charter
    would have held because the situation could not arise — an unfalsifiable
    rule. The aggressor's company marches with its host; the defender's holds its
    gate.
    AND A HARD CRASH ON THE WAY THROUGH. The world tick's necromancer loop reads
    `hm.x` off `c.roamNecro ? c : c.homeTown`, and a guild necromancer standing in
    a hall has neither — it took the whole frame with it on the first run. Guarded,
    and the yard is now a third answer to "whose ground is this one working".

[DONE] New spell: Old Bones. Reanimates a small group of temporary undead
soldiers from long dead bones. They only last one day while the old magic holds
them together, but are useful in a pinch.
    Proof in tools/hosts.js, which covers both new spells.
    Dark, tier 2, magic 10, 16 mana, six tiles. The one raise in the game that
    needs NO CORPSE — nine generations of Fracture and a golden age before it put
    a great deal into this ground, and none of it is identifiable any more. Two
    to five of them by magic, thin (34 blood, 40 in the vitals), armed with a
    club or nothing.
    NOTHING IS BOUND, WHICH IS THE WHOLE MECHANIC. A necromancer whose binding is
    already full can still cast it — that is the "in a pinch" the report asks for
    — and what stops it being free is that you get ONE DAY and cannot bank them.
    Cast it before the fight or waste it.
    IT DOES NOT DIE, IT STOPS. `crumble` and deliberately not `kill()`: a thing
    held up by a working that ran out leaves no corpse, so there is nothing to
    harvest, nothing to raise a second time, and no death for the squad to have a
    view about. `kill` posts to the deed ledger and moves everyone's regard, and
    a host you were always going to lose on schedule is not a loss. Without that,
    the spell is a corpse printer.
    Hallowed ground refuses it, as it refuses everything else.

[DONE] New spell: Mass Reanimation. Raise all dead creatures in a large radius.
They last three days, but consume the majority of the caster's concentration.
The casting itself is an extensive, time-consuming ritual.
    Proof in tools/hosts.js.
    Dark, tier 3, magic 22, 34 mana, fourteen tiles. Three separate costs and
    all three are load-bearing:
      · THE RITE. Rooted and defenceless, 45 seconds at magic 40, built on the
        Last Rite's shape — work accrues at a rate CAPPED by magic, so skill
        shortens the vigil rather than deleting it. That cap is not decoration:
        the Last Rite was six and a half seconds long for a year because an
        uncapped magic multiplier ate a fixed-length job, and that lesson is
        written into this one rather than relearned.
      · THE HEAD ROOM. 65% of the caster's pool, locked for as long as the host
        stands — the largest concentration lock in the game against the warding's
        1.0-but-exclusive and the burning edge's 0.5. Measured: 55 down to 19.
      · THE CLOCK. Three days, then the whole host goes.
    LETTING GO DISMISSES THEM, and that is a release valve rather than a
    punishment. The spell is a decision to spend three days as a smaller caster
    with a bigger army, and the player has to be able to end it and get their
    pool back.
    THE HARNESS FOUND A REAL ONE, and it is the kind nothing else would have.
    `risenLoad` sums `(x.bindWeight || 1)`, and both spells set `bindWeight = 0`
    — so `0 || 1` put every propped body straight back on the binding. Measured:
    twenty mass-raised bodies reading as twenty slots off a ceiling of seven,
    which is the entire cost model of the dark art quietly inverted. No outcome
    test could see it: the army works, the spell works, the ceiling is just gone.
    Fixed by the `propped` flag at the three places the ceiling actually lives
    (`risenLoad`, `bindLoads`, `upkeepBodies`) rather than by chasing `|| 1`
    through eleven call sites — `propped` is the question that was actually being
    asked. `hostSize` deliberately still counts them: twenty of the dead walking
    beside you is twenty as far as the world is concerned, however you are
    holding them.
    AND THE LIST IS SNAPSHOTTED BEFORE ANYTHING IS RAISED, because `castRaise`
    splices out of `corpses` — filtering and raising in one pass walks a
    shrinking array and skips every other body, which would have read as the
    radius being half what it says.
    `castRaise` gained an options bag ({free, temp, quiet}) and now returns the
    risen rather than `true`, so a caller raising twenty can stamp all twenty.
    Still truthy for every existing caller.
    AND raise.js WENT RED ON THE WAY THROUGH, on a hand-copied list of the four
    chimera lines that named `thinblood` — cut two commits earlier. It reported
    "A RAISED CHIMERA LOSES ITS LINE" about a line that no longer exists, which
    is a probe telling you about itself. It reads `Object.keys(SUBRACES.chimera)`
    now.

[DONE] Six harnesses went red in the full suite at the end of this batch, and two
of them were the game. (Not reported — found by running the suite.)
    Every one of the six is written up here because the diagnosis is the useful
    half, and because four of them are the same fault in four costumes: a probe
    that let something it was not controlling into its measurement, and then went
    red the day fourteen bodies were added to worldgen a thousand lines above it.
    THE TWO REAL ONES FIRST.
    · A CENTRED CLICK MISSES A BODY ON A SLOPE. `storeyHit` iterates its ground
      plane against the terrain — its own comment explains why, "a cave under a
      mountain does not [stand on flat ground], and the plane came out wherever
      sea level would have been" — and `aimGround`, the other half of the same
      question, was still `h / tan(pitch)`, which is the flat-ground answer. The
      two agreed exactly as long as the body stood somewhere level. The worldgen
      shift moved cave.js's cave from ground falling 0.40 a tile to ground
      falling 0.83, and a click aimed dead centre at a body three storeys down
      resolved 1.32 tiles away — wider than any catchment in the file, so it
      could not be selected at all. Nothing about picking had changed; the ground
      under the test had.
      The ray falls the body's height PLUS however far the ground drops away over
      the distance it travels. Solved by the same fixed point `storeyHit` uses,
      three passes, on a click rather than on a frame. 1.32 tiles to 0.47.
      cave.js now prints the residual AND the local slope, because a yes/no
      cannot tell you it passed with nothing to spare — this had been passing at
      0.32 against a catchment it exceeded at 1.32, and a number would have shown
      the drift instead of flipping.
    · A BODY OVER YOUR SHOULDER COULD NOT BE PUT DOWN. The carried-body branch of
      the right-click handler sat above the ENEMY branch, on the sound reasoning
      that somebody you are rescuing must not be offered EXECUTE. Sound, and two
      hundred lines too low: a carried body stands at the BEARER'S OWN POSITION,
      so the squadmate branch and the friendly-local branch — both above it —
      were looking at two bodies on one tile and the click went to whichever of
      the pair sorted nearer. A sub-tile coin flip, and it flipped: the click
      started offering GUARD and COURT and there was no way left to set anybody
      down. It is the first thing asked now. Nothing on that tile can be more
      specific than what to do with the holding.
    AND THE FOUR PROBE FAULTS, WHICH ARE ONE FAULT.
    · melee.js set `blood = 1e6` to make its bodies unkillable, and that is not
      what unkillable means: `updateState` drops anybody whose VITAL PARTS are at
      or below zero whatever is in the veins. Six katana-armed bandits put the
      runner on the ground in ninety ticks, `physics` calls `clearOrders` on a
      body going down — wiping the order the whole case is about — and the loop
      then skips it forever. `orderNotSuicide` was reading whether the runner
      happened to switch target BEFORE it was knocked out. The parts go up with
      the blood now and the fight runs to the end.
    · survive.js and melee.js both staged bodies with `makeChar` and no race, so
      every one of them rolled a human LINE — and a line carries stats. Salt-cured
      is +3 tough +3 armour; Ironscar-bred is +3 atk +3 blades. A hundred and
      forty-four bodies through a twelve-round skirmish is a different mix every
      time the stream moves: 16 of 43 died before, 25 of 48 after, both perfectly
      correct, one of them under a 50% floor. Pinned to Dustborn, the line with
      no bonuses and no gaps, which is what a question about the BLEED MODEL
      wants under it.
    · watchers.js compared two Messengers on "an identical starting line" and the
      comment was right about what the control needed while the code did not
      deliver it — `spawnGaunt` goes through `makeChar`, so one came up Ironscar-
      bred and the other Orchard-bred. `xpGain` reads BOTH `learnMult` and the
      subrace's per-skill `learn`, so levelling the starting number alone was not
      enough either. Both now equalised by hand.
    · names.js asked its golem question of whatever golems the world happened to
      contain, and golems are 1.8% of what walks — one on the first run, none on
      the second, and it reported "THERE ARE NO GOLEMS TO CHECK" about a naming
      convention that works. A probe whose subject may not exist is not a probe;
      the claim is asked of the namer directly now, twelve times.
    AND ONE MEASUREMENT ERROR OF MY OWN, worth writing down: cave.js does not use
    the `***` summary convention the other files do, so `grep -c '^\*\*\*'`
    counted zero for three consecutive FAILING runs and I read it as passing.
    Check what a file actually prints before counting it.

