[DONE] Sometimes the "displayed" conviction an NPC has at the bar changes after
they get hired.
    Proof in tools/hire.js. 23 of 29 hires changed conviction on the build
    before; 0 after.
    THE ROW IS A CONTRACT. It is the one place in this game where you are shown a
    person's character before you pay for them, and it is the whole reason one
    hand costs more than another — a Compassionate medic and a Cruel one are two
    different runs. `makeRecruit` rolled a conviction, the row printed it, and the
    till threw it away: the hire called `makeChar` without it and `makeChar` rolls
    its own. Measured across every board in the world, twenty-nine rows: twenty-
    three of them handed over somebody else.
    AND THE STATS WERE LYING TOO, WHICH NOBODY REPORTED, because they are off by
    ones and twos rather than by a word. `makeChar` adds the LINE's flat gifts on
    top of whatever the caller asked for and `makeRecruit` returns the roll from
    before that, so a hand advertised at atk 9 arrived at 8 and one at tough 10
    arrived at 13. Twenty-two of twenty-nine. The line's gifts are not the bug —
    that is how lines work — the ROW was printing the wrong half of the sum.
    `recruitStats()` computes it the way `makeChar` will, and the row asks it, so
    the two cannot drift apart again.
    AND THE DRAW IS SPENT EITHER WAY. `o.conviction || rollConviction(_gift)`
    short-circuits the roll for any caller that names one, which is rule 2 in that
    file's own notes and started mattering the moment the bar began passing one:
    buying a hand would have cost the world one fewer number than not buying them.
    THE PROBE HAD TO BE STOPPED FROM MEASURING THE SQUAD CAP. `bannerLoad() >=
    SQUAD_CAP` refuses the tenth hire, and nine rows is small enough that a
    re-rolled conviction landing on the advertised one by luck is a real risk —
    one in eight per row. Each body is measured and then stood down out of the
    banner, which is what took the sample from 9 to 29.

[DONE] We need a cooldown for blood moon events too. Getting them several times
in a row is rough.
    Proof in tools/nights.js. 75 of 194 pairs inside a fortnight before; 0 of 168
    after.
    THE MEAN WAS NEVER THE COMPLAINT. Measured over six thousand nights on the
    build that shipped it: 195 red moons, one every thirty-one nights, which is
    exactly the "once in a long while" the design note above the function asks
    for. The gaps are the problem — median 20, longest 187, and a SHORTEST OF
    TWO, because the only thing between one red moon and the next was the single
    morning the roll spent clearing the flag. An independent coin flipped every
    dawn has no shortest gap; clustering is not a flaw in that design, it IS that
    design.
    A FLOOR, THEN A RAMP, NOT A HARD COOLDOWN. Sixteen nights of nothing, then
