[SUITE] 99/99, after three runs and one more lesson than expected. The first came back 96/99 — focus.js, press.js and sixfold.js —
and the three of them are one story worth writing down in full, because ONE of them was a real
regression I would not have found any other way and the other two were the harness lying.
    THE REGRESSION: THE NEW PATHING FALLBACK WAS PULLING BODIES OUT OF FIGHTS. Inside a few
    tiles of its goal a body stops making progress all the time and none of it is a pathing
    failure — it is at the target and the target is surrounded, or its own line is in the
    doorway, or it is trading blows. My stuck detector counted that as stuck and sent it on a
    perpendicular detour, which is not a rescue, it is a retreat. press.js went from twelve
    bodies on six sides of an enemy to four, the Sixfold's stamp stopped finding four bodies
    crowded together, and focus.js watched its line come apart. Every symptom a MOVEMENT
    symptom and none of them anywhere near a file I had opened. `STUCK_NEAR = 4` — the fallback
    is for journeys, and within four tiles the crowd sorts itself out — plus a three-second
    cadence and the detour held back to the third failure. All three green again.
    THE FIRST LIE: A THRESHOLD FITTED TO ONE LUCKY RUN. sixfold's `oneBlowTakesTheRank` was
    `on >= 8` against a measured 9 — a margin of ONE on three trials of six bodies — and it went
    red on a change with nothing to do with the Sixfold. Raising a sundered site's population
    from 2-4 to 5-8 draws more `rnd()` AT WORLDGEN, every draw after it moves, and the world the
    ring settles into is a different world. Proved by reverting that one line and watching 6 go
    back to 9. Six trials now, and the claim restated as what the section is actually about —
    more than one body a blow: 2.00 against a control of 0.83.
    THE SECOND LIE WAS THE SAME DISEASE AND MUCH WORSE. sixfold's battle A/B never reset the
    seed, in a file whose own comment records that lesson one section above. So the two arms ran
    from wherever the stream happened to be — and adding three trials upstream moved it from 12
    kills against 0 to 0 against 0 with no game code changing at all. Given the SAME dice it read
    2 against 1 on this build and 0 against 1 on the build BEFORE the batch, which means the
    17-against-0 the Sixfold was originally tuned against was an artefact of the two arms rolling
    different dice. Four seeds now, each arm run on all four, claim made on the totals: 50 bodies
    of 96 against 9, and 245 blood against 202. The arsenal does work — it was never visible in
    a single sixty-second world.

[NOTE] A SEEDED STREAM IS A SHARED MUTABLE AND WORLDGEN IS PART OF IT. Adding two spawns to a
site moved a beast's sweep three bodies. Anything that draws during worldgen re-deals the entire
world, so an absolute number measured downstream is a number about that particular deal — the
only durable claims are RATIOS against a control run on the same dice, and the only way to get
the same dice is to set the seed yourself at the top of each arm.

[NOTE] AND A CHAOTIC END-TO-END NUMBER NEEDS MORE THAN ONE WORLD. "How many of twenty-four die
in sixty seconds" swings on one early swing changing who is standing where for the rest of the
minute; one seed said 2 against 1, four seeds say 50 against 9. When an A/B on matched dice
comes back nearly flat, the question to ask is whether the sample is one deal, before concluding
the feature does nothing.

[NOTE] AND THE SECOND RUN FOUND A FOURTH ONE: 98/99, world.js, `cairnExists`. It read the global
`cairn` at ONE INSTANT — after a fixed nine thousand ticks, a cull of every wild animal, and four
thousand more ticks — and asked whether a beast was breathing right then. That is a coin flip
dressed as an assertion. The Cairn Beast is neutral fauna in a world where the Purge and the
Bastion both hunt it, because they were TAUGHT to on purpose two batches ago; whether one happens
to be alive at that particular moment turns on a fortnight of chaotic combat, and a pathing change
that altered how bodies walk was enough to flip it. Measured: a clean run to day twelve has one
standing on BOTH builds, and the beast is seeded on day 1 — so it was never a question of whether
it spawns.
    WHAT THE SECTION IS FOR is that the beast gets into the world and is announced with a place
    on it: "it always spawned, it was five hundred tiles away, and a whole playthrough never met
    it." So it watches for one AS THE RUN HAPPENS and reports the day it appeared, and says
    plainly when something has since killed it — which is the world working, not a failure.
    Findability is still covered, by `cairnSaysWhere` and `cairnBearing`, which never wavered.

[NOTE] FOUR ASSERTIONS IN ONE BATCH FAILED FOR THE SAME UNDERLYING REASON, and it is worth
naming as one thing rather than four: A CHAOTIC SIMULATION READ AT ONE POINT IS NOT A
MEASUREMENT. A threshold fitted to one lucky ring (sixfold's 9 with a bound of 8), an A/B whose
two arms rolled different dice (sixfold's battle), a kill count over one sixty-second world, and
a snapshot of one creature after a fortnight of combat. Every one of them went red on a change
that had nothing to do with what it was testing, and every one was fixed in the HARNESS rather
than the game. The tell each time was the same: the mechanism's own direct measurements — bodies
per blow, six of six caught by the stamp, three of three by one throw — never moved at all.

